    "description": "Iran's pharmaceutical and medical-equipment sector connects manufacturing, regulation, procurement, and clinical services. Understanding it requires distinguishing the production of medicines from access to treatment, and the delivery of equipment from its sustained operation.",
    "tags": ["Pharmaceuticals", "Medical equipment", "Manufacturing", "Quality assurance", "Healthcare access"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:16:19Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:16:19Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Pharmaceuticals and Medical Equipment in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran's medicine and medical-equipment sector, from manufacturing and quality assurance to procurement, maintenance, and access."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "sector-overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From industrial production to patient care",
        "content": "Iran's medicines and medical-equipment sector is best understood as a chain of different capabilities. Producing a medicine, checking its quality, distributing it, and making it available to a patient are separate achievements. Similarly, purchasing a scanner does not by itself establish that a hospital can keep it operating. The distinction is visible in both pharmaceutical research and equipment procurement documents: a medicine-availability study examines what pharmacies actually hold, while a World Health Organization tender for Iran explicitly requires installation, training, and after-sales support. [s1][s2]\n\nThis branch therefore separates pharmaceutical companies, raw materials, medical equipment, quality-control laboratories, pharmaceutical supply chains, and diagnostic-equipment manufacturing. These subjects overlap, but each asks a different question: who makes the product, what inputs it needs, how its performance is verified, and how it reaches a functioning health service.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "quality-systems",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Quality extends beyond the laboratory",
        "content": "Good manufacturing practice, commonly abbreviated to GMP, is a system for producing and controlling medicines consistently. WHO describes it as covering documented and validated processes, suitable premises and equipment, appropriate materials, and trained personnel. It also encompasses responsibilities involving contract work, distribution, defects, and complaints. A laboratory result is one part of this wider system; it cannot replace control of how a product was manufactured. [s3]\n\nFor readers comparing manufacturers, this makes the scope of a quality claim important. A statement about a factory, product, or inspection should be read in its specific context. WHO's pharmaceutical-quality compendium provides international guidance for manufacturers and regulators; its existence does not certify any particular Iranian company or medicine. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "equipment-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Medical equipment needs a service network",
        "content": "A WHO invitation to bid issued for Iran in July 2020 illustrates the practical requirements behind an equipment purchase. It required detailed technical specifications, manufacturer and country-of-origin information, warranty provisions, Iranian Food and Drug Administration registration, and local agents able to provide installation, training, and after-sales services. These were conditions of that procurement, rather than proof that every supplier in Iran meets them. [s2]\n\nGeography also matters. WHO reported in October 2023 that computed tomography scanners had been delivered to public hospitals in Hamedan, Ilam, Mazandaran, North Khorasan, Razavi Khorasan, and Yazd. Computed tomography, or CT, produces cross-sectional images using X-rays. The announcement demonstrates a particular distribution of equipment beyond the capital; it does not establish equal access across provinces, staffing adequacy, or subsequent uptime. Its larger equipment programme was described partly in future tense, so planned deliveries should not be counted as completed installations. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "policy-versus-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read policy commitments separately from results",
        "content": "An English-language development-plan extract hosted by WHO groups medicines and medical equipment within a shared supply agenda. Article 71 addresses strategic reserves, supply-chain settlements, product authentication, pricing, and industrial renewal. Its language describes duties and intended changes. It does not demonstrate that reserves were accumulated, payments settled, or tracing systems completed. The extract's legal provenance and implementation dates need confirmation against authoritative Iranian texts before it can support a current legal account. [s6]\n\nThe useful conclusion is therefore practical: assess industrial capability, quality assurance, purchasing, and service delivery separately. Evidence of domestic production is informative, but a fuller assessment also needs product-specific quality records, reliable supply observations, and evidence that installed equipment remains usable. This page provides that framework; a comprehensive account of the sector's current scale remains unfinished.",
        "source_ids": ["s6"]
      }
    ],
